Visual-only
* **Play With Movement:** Zoom in/out, pan up/down, jump, drop the camera towards your subject and test that footage slow mo. Anything that catches the eye. Editing could achieve some similar, but that would bring us back to the ~rapid montage~
* **Unexpected:** Create a visual that is not typically seen. Play with texture, throw your product at the camera, smash it, squeeze it, bite/eat something unexpected. Just make the person wonder what the heck is up (for even 1 second) and you may have them hooked.
* **Unique Color Palette:** Break through the noise with something as simple as uncommon coloring.
* **Confuse Viewers:** Get weird with b-roll and take some of those strange shoots, chop them up, and make them 
* **Organic Trends:** I'm repeating a bit with this one, but also not. Take your existing content and shoot a trending visual rather than relying on a popular sound OR breaking down a common organic hook format (i.e., food jutsu)
* **Avatar On-Screen Placement:" If your video features a person, pet, or other creature, consider the different angles/crops/lenses/camera focus/body language/scenarios/costume/light effects/action you could test to capture attention. This is one of the ways you can most play with your ads. Having a person on screen usually improves performance compared to content that doesn't feature anyone. However, there are so many different ways people can come across, and even beyond a "persona" try to take a step back and imagine your creators as actors, and the content as a stage/scene. What could you do? Possibilites are pretty much endless.

Verbal-only
* **Storytime:** Start telling a story. You don't have to actually say "Storytime" or use the on caption "POV" -- get inventive with it!
* **Organic Keywords:** Look at TikTok or your search term reports and pick out some of the most common phrases, pay close attention to the specific wording. Make those your hooks. This could work with a problem/solution video, a product introduction video, UGC social proof/storytelling, or even Authority. If you're saying the very things mouch of your audience is thinking or researching, in the exact way they would phrase it, they're more likely to stick around and find out what comes next.
* **How To:** This naturally pairs with explainer style content. It will cover how to do a relevant topic. That could be using your product/service, solving a problem your business addresses, or try something new. If someone is interested in learning to do something better/at home, they'll watch to see how they can be successful.
* **Bestie Recommends:** This will usually be UGC or an Influencer video, introducing something as a fave, or the best, or must-have

Can you tell how much fun I've had with these, and how fun it could be for you too? Once you narrow in on one brand or business, you can really let your creativity go wild. Honestly, brainstorm combos in batches, produce visuals in batches, source content in batches then repeat the process again and again.

Outside of defined personas and organic feed content, how else can you unlock new hook angles for testing? So many ways:
* **Scour reviews (yours and competitors)** 
* **Look at creator sales content (LTK, TikTok Shop)** 
* **Keep an eye on videos organically recommended in your feed (not organic content you've searched for based on keywords)** 


